1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,800
Your name is Noah.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,400
And you remember...

3
00:00:03,767 --> 00:00:05,067
every single day.

4
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:07,800
You close your eyes...

5
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:09,600
and you are there.

6
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:13,767
Everything you just watched started as a

7
00:00:13,767 --> 00:00:14,700
plain script.

8
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:15,967
In this video,

9
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,867
we'll take one original idea,

10
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:19,800
shape it into a story with one

11
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:20,633
clear rule,

12
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:23,133
turn that story into scenes that actually

13
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:23,967
make sense,

14
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,233
and finish with a real video file.

15
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:27,633
Stay to the end,

16
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:29,867
and you'll see the finished scene.

17
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:31,200
First,

18
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:32,300
the inspiration:

19
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:34,333
three channels that make short,

20
00:00:34,367 --> 00:00:36,033
narrated animated stories.

21
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:38,233
Ifverse is our main reference.

22
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:39,667
Every video asks:

23
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:41,500
what if your own body or mind

24
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:42,767
worked differently?

25
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:44,867
Day One Films tells compact,

26
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:46,100
emotional stories,

27
00:00:46,133 --> 00:00:47,633
and so does Last Soul.

28
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:49,800
We can see what they publish,

29
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:51,267
not how they make it,

30
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:53,233
so we borrow the format only.

31
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:54,267
Our story,

32
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:55,333
our characters,

33
00:00:55,367 --> 00:00:57,500
and our visuals are all original.

34
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:00,367
This format has four ingredients.

35
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:01,900
One clear question:

36
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:04,500
what if you remembered every single day?

37
00:01:04,733 --> 00:01:06,500
A consequence you can see:

38
00:01:06,533 --> 00:01:08,900
Noah's memories keep pulling him out of

39
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:09,767
the present.

40
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:11,367
An emotional choice,

41
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,500
between the past and the friend in

42
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:14,467
front of him.

43
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:16,667
And a payoff that calls back to

44
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:17,467
the start:

45
00:01:17,467 --> 00:01:18,767
this blue notebook.

46
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,533
Here's the whole route.

47
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:21,767
Pick a topic,

48
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:23,000
write the script,

49
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:24,400
and check the facts.

50
00:01:24,633 --> 00:01:26,133
Then record the voice,

51
00:01:26,167 --> 00:01:27,767
and plan scenes from it.

52
00:01:28,033 --> 00:01:28,533
Next:

53
00:01:28,533 --> 00:01:29,333
characters,

54
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:29,967
motion,

55
00:01:29,967 --> 00:01:30,767
and sound.

56
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:31,633
Finally,

57
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:32,867
review and export.

58
00:01:33,133 --> 00:01:35,500
The story and voice are finished before

59
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:36,333
we animate.

60
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:38,000
Three tools,

61
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:38,833
three jobs.

62
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:40,633
Claude writes the code,

63
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:42,533
Remotion turns it into video,

64
00:01:42,567 --> 00:01:44,567
and ElevenLabs makes the voice.

65
00:01:44,833 --> 00:01:47,500
Remotion's official guide covers the setup.

66
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:50,200
These commands create a blank project,

67
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:52,733
add Remotion's skills for coding agents,

68
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:54,267
and start the preview.

69
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,167
In a second terminal,

70
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:00,633
open Claude Code inside the same project

71
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:01,433
folder.

72
00:02:01,467 --> 00:02:03,233
Now Claude edits the files,

73
00:02:03,267 --> 00:02:05,333
and you watch every change here.

74
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:09,033
Let's start with the idea.

75
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:11,700
I paste the topic prompt into Claude.

76
00:02:11,967 --> 00:02:12,900
Its key rule:

77
00:02:12,933 --> 00:02:15,367
change exactly one thing about your body

78
00:02:15,367 --> 00:02:16,067
or mind,

79
00:02:16,067 --> 00:02:17,800
and push it to an extreme.

80
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:19,700
Claude sends back titles.

81
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:22,033
Pick one that could carry a whole

82
00:02:22,033 --> 00:02:22,833
story.

83
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,033
Inspired by these,

84
00:02:25,067 --> 00:02:25,833
I picked:

85
00:02:25,833 --> 00:02:28,367
what if you remembered every single day?

86
00:02:28,633 --> 00:02:30,233
It has real consequences,

87
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:32,033
and it can hang on one small

88
00:02:32,033 --> 00:02:33,300
relationship detail.

89
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:34,600
Next,

90
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:35,833
the script prompt.

91
00:02:36,067 --> 00:02:38,300
I add the topic and three anchors:

92
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:38,867
Noah,

93
00:02:38,867 --> 00:02:39,367
Mila,

94
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:40,733
and a blue notebook.

95
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,300
Claude writes the full example film;

96
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:45,167
we'll only show pieces here.

97
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:46,767
First,

98
00:02:46,767 --> 00:02:47,767
find the rule.

99
00:02:48,033 --> 00:02:49,700
Noah can relive the past,

100
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:51,833
but he can never predict or change

101
00:02:51,833 --> 00:02:52,433
it.

102
00:02:52,433 --> 00:02:53,867
Then find the choice:

103
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:54,633
the past,

104
00:02:54,633 --> 00:02:56,567
or the friend right in front of

105
00:02:56,567 --> 00:02:57,233
him.

106
00:02:57,233 --> 00:02:57,733
Hook,

107
00:02:57,733 --> 00:02:58,233
rule,

108
00:02:58,233 --> 00:02:59,100
consequences,

109
00:02:59,133 --> 00:02:59,767
choice,

110
00:02:59,767 --> 00:03:00,567
callback.

111
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,333
That's the story's spine.

112
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:05,467
Before recording,

113
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:06,633
check the facts.

114
00:03:06,867 --> 00:03:08,300
The power is fiction,

115
00:03:08,333 --> 00:03:10,033
so we label it as the story

116
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:10,833
premise.

117
00:03:10,933 --> 00:03:13,200
But the script also makes real claims

118
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:14,733
about how memory works,

119
00:03:14,767 --> 00:03:16,933
and my first draft overstated one.

120
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,533
So find a reliable source,

121
00:03:19,567 --> 00:03:21,500
ideally the original research,

122
00:03:22,333 --> 00:03:24,533
and change the wording until it says

123
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:26,533
only what that source supports.

124
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:28,633
Claims checked,

125
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:29,833
premise labelled.

126
00:03:30,067 --> 00:03:31,933
Now the script is ready for a

127
00:03:31,933 --> 00:03:32,733
voice.

128
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:34,200
In ElevenLabs,

129
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:36,067
paste only the spoken words.

130
00:03:36,333 --> 00:03:37,200
No headings,

131
00:03:37,233 --> 00:03:38,033
no notes.

132
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:40,733
Choose one voice and one model,

133
00:03:40,767 --> 00:03:42,900
and keep them for the whole film.

134
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:45,233
Listen to a short sample first.

135
00:03:45,533 --> 00:03:47,433
This little film started as a plain

136
00:03:47,433 --> 00:03:48,233
script.

137
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:50,900
When it sounds right,

138
00:03:50,933 --> 00:03:53,367
generate the narration and save the real

139
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:54,200
audio file.

140
00:03:55,067 --> 00:03:57,133
Those pauses now set the rhythm.

141
00:03:57,367 --> 00:03:59,467
Every cut we plan will follow this

142
00:03:59,467 --> 00:04:00,267
recording.

143
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,133
The script is one long paragraph,

144
00:04:03,167 --> 00:04:05,233
but animation needs short beats.

145
00:04:05,467 --> 00:04:08,033
So split it wherever the action changes.

146
00:04:08,267 --> 00:04:09,867
Each beat gets a start,

147
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:10,533
an end,

148
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:11,333
its words,

149
00:04:11,333 --> 00:04:12,400
and one action.

150
00:04:12,633 --> 00:04:14,467
When the narration moves on,

151
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,167
the picture moves on too.

152
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:18,567
Every beat links one spoken line to

153
00:04:18,567 --> 00:04:20,033
one thing we can see.

154
00:04:20,267 --> 00:04:22,133
Line them up over the audio,

155
00:04:22,167 --> 00:04:23,800
and you have a timeline.

156
00:04:24,067 --> 00:04:26,000
Sentences can run across cuts.

157
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:27,233
Now,

158
00:04:27,233 --> 00:04:28,267
the characters.

159
00:04:28,533 --> 00:04:29,467
This is Noah.

160
00:04:29,733 --> 00:04:31,433
Three things never change:

161
00:04:31,467 --> 00:04:32,933
his terracotta jacket,

162
00:04:32,967 --> 00:04:34,033
his proportions,

163
00:04:34,067 --> 00:04:35,567
and his blue notebook.

164
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:38,233
Claude builds him from reusable parts,

165
00:04:38,267 --> 00:04:39,867
so a new pose is the same

166
00:04:39,867 --> 00:04:41,533
Noah doing something new.

167
00:04:41,767 --> 00:04:43,467
His expression can change.

168
00:04:43,733 --> 00:04:44,900
His design can't.

169
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:47,000
Mila follows the same rules,

170
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:48,900
so both stay in scale in every

171
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:49,667
scene.

172
00:04:49,667 --> 00:04:50,167
Next,

173
00:04:50,167 --> 00:04:50,967
the place.

174
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,233
Every scene is built in layers.

175
00:04:53,467 --> 00:04:54,367
At the back:

176
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:55,267
the windows,

177
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:56,067
the wall,

178
00:04:56,067 --> 00:04:57,167
and a slow sky.

179
00:04:57,433 --> 00:04:58,433
In the middle:

180
00:04:58,467 --> 00:04:59,667
the action layer,

181
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:01,867
where the characters sit and move.

182
00:05:02,133 --> 00:05:03,067
And in front,

183
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:04,167
the foreground.

184
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:05,900
When the camera moves,

185
00:05:05,933 --> 00:05:08,300
near things shift more than far ones.

186
00:05:08,567 --> 00:05:09,833
That creates depth.

187
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:11,967
Put the layers back together,

188
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,067
and the same style works inside a

189
00:05:14,067 --> 00:05:14,900
memory too.

190
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:16,133
Now,

191
00:05:16,133 --> 00:05:17,633
motion with a purpose.

192
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:20,167
Noah is about to open his notebook.

193
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:21,833
Watch for five beats:

194
00:05:21,867 --> 00:05:22,467
notice,

195
00:05:22,467 --> 00:05:23,033
reach,

196
00:05:23,033 --> 00:05:23,567
touch,

197
00:05:23,567 --> 00:05:24,067
open,

198
00:05:24,067 --> 00:05:24,867
react.

199
00:05:30,333 --> 00:05:32,867
The hand stays attached and really touches

200
00:05:32,867 --> 00:05:33,667
the cover.

201
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:34,933
Nothing floats.

202
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:37,600
Anticipation before the move,

203
00:05:37,633 --> 00:05:39,133
contact in the middle,

204
00:05:39,167 --> 00:05:41,167
and a small settle at the end.

205
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:43,000
That night,

206
00:05:43,067 --> 00:05:45,633
you open the notebook one last time.

207
00:05:46,467 --> 00:05:48,800
And every movement belongs to the line

208
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:49,800
it sits under.

209
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:52,200
Here's a timing mistake.

210
00:05:52,467 --> 00:05:53,733
Watch the notebook.

211
00:05:54,067 --> 00:05:54,967
That night,

212
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:57,600
you open the notebook one last time.

213
00:05:58,733 --> 00:06:01,300
It opens before the words describing it.

214
00:06:01,533 --> 00:06:03,667
Find that phrase in the waveform.

215
00:06:03,933 --> 00:06:06,033
Ask Claude to move the action onto

216
00:06:06,033 --> 00:06:06,833
that cue.

217
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,033
The audio itself doesn't change.

218
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:10,567
Now play it again.

219
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:11,800
That night,

220
00:06:11,833 --> 00:06:14,433
you open the notebook one last time.

221
00:06:15,567 --> 00:06:16,367
Same voice,

222
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:17,333
same drawing.

223
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:19,200
Only the timing changed,

224
00:06:19,233 --> 00:06:20,767
and now it feels right.

225
00:06:22,367 --> 00:06:22,867
Next,

226
00:06:22,867 --> 00:06:23,633
sound.

227
00:06:23,633 --> 00:06:25,500
Here's the voice on its own.

228
00:06:25,767 --> 00:06:27,667
This little film started as a plain

229
00:06:27,667 --> 00:06:28,467
script.

230
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:31,200
Add quiet café ambience,

231
00:06:31,233 --> 00:06:32,833
and the room feels real.

232
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:34,533
Then one paper sound,

233
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:36,433
exactly when the cover opens.

234
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:38,600
And a soft music bed that dips

235
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:40,167
whenever someone speaks.

236
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:41,767
Here's the full mix.

237
00:06:42,033 --> 00:06:43,500
The voice still leads.

238
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:45,700
This little film started as a plain

239
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:46,500
script.

240
00:06:47,833 --> 00:06:50,200
Now review the draft at normal speed,

241
00:06:50,233 --> 00:06:51,733
and look for problems.

242
00:06:51,967 --> 00:06:54,333
Here's a real one from this project:

243
00:06:54,367 --> 00:06:56,467
Noah's legs were drawn in front of

244
00:06:56,467 --> 00:06:57,267
the table,

245
00:06:57,267 --> 00:06:58,633
instead of under it.

246
00:06:59,667 --> 00:07:01,433
Describe the fix precisely:

247
00:07:01,467 --> 00:07:02,400
what's wrong,

248
00:07:02,433 --> 00:07:03,700
what should happen,

249
00:07:03,733 --> 00:07:05,600
and what must stay the same.

250
00:07:06,667 --> 00:07:08,400
Claude edits the component,

251
00:07:08,433 --> 00:07:10,367
and we render the shot again.

252
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,000
Same shot,

253
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:12,867
same timing.

254
00:07:13,133 --> 00:07:15,300
Now his legs sit under the table.

255
00:07:16,133 --> 00:07:19,133
Because we fixed the character component itself,

256
00:07:19,167 --> 00:07:21,633
every shot that reuses it improves too.

257
00:07:22,433 --> 00:07:23,733
When review passes,

258
00:07:23,767 --> 00:07:26,567
check the render settings you actually chose.

259
00:07:27,433 --> 00:07:29,567
Run the render and let it finish.

260
00:07:29,833 --> 00:07:31,000
We'll skip ahead.

261
00:07:31,867 --> 00:07:33,567
Then open the actual file.

262
00:07:34,633 --> 00:07:35,967
Check the beginning,

263
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:36,833
the middle,

264
00:07:36,867 --> 00:07:37,767
and the end:

265
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:38,467
picture,

266
00:07:38,467 --> 00:07:39,033
voice,

267
00:07:39,033 --> 00:07:39,833
and timing.

268
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,367
All three pass.

269
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:45,900
Here's the finished scene.

270
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:48,133
On Monday,

271
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:49,967
you're back at the café.

272
00:07:50,267 --> 00:07:52,800
Mila is telling you about her week.

273
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:55,000
But you're somewhere else.

274
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:56,167
A Tuesday,

275
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:57,567
three summers ago...

276
00:07:57,967 --> 00:07:59,200
this same table.

277
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:00,400
Same laugh.

278
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:02,700
Then two taps on the table.

279
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:03,800
Noah...

280
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:05,300
are you even here?

281
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:07,433
You look at the notebook.

282
00:08:07,733 --> 00:08:09,067
And you close it.

283
00:08:09,367 --> 00:08:11,433
For the first time in weeks...

284
00:08:11,833 --> 00:08:12,733
you listen.

285
00:08:14,167 --> 00:08:15,000
One script,

286
00:08:15,033 --> 00:08:15,833
one voice,

287
00:08:15,833 --> 00:08:17,467
and one consistent world.

288
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:19,533
That's one finished example.

289
00:08:20,667 --> 00:08:22,033
For your next video,

290
00:08:22,067 --> 00:08:23,233
change the topic,

291
00:08:23,267 --> 00:08:25,167
rewrite and check the script,

292
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:26,367
record the voice,

293
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:27,900
and retime the scenes.

294
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:30,467
So which ability would you try next?

295
00:08:30,733 --> 00:08:32,333
Tell me in the comments.

296
00:08:33,367 --> 00:08:34,733
Thanks for watching.

